+## Phone briefing: two tiles per row on every front — November 7, 2026 (America/Chicago)
+
+Commit: phone briefing on News, Sports and Entertainment uses Tech's two-per-row grid (`.nf-brief--phone .nf-brief__tiles` in `sections.css`); an odd last tile spans the row (Sports' Sports news, Entertainment's Tonight on TV). Word values break between words (`overflow-wrap: break-word`) and size with `clamp(20px, 6vw, 26px)`, replacing the 22px rule under 360px. Tech's own rule in `mobile.css` stays for the Tech week summary. Desktop and tablet unchanged (tablets hide `.nf-brief--phone`). New assertions in `section-fronts.browser.cjs` and `news-front.browser.cjs`.
+
+Verification (authoring session, static render against the real stylesheets): 320, 390 and 430px in light and dark, all four fronts two per row, odd last tile full width, no sideways scroll, no value overflowing its tile, screenshots inspected. `check-css` and Prettier passed. Not run there (no .NET SDK): `tools/verify.sh`, the browser checks against the app, deploy and `live-check`. Run these when applying.
+
 ## Sports and Entertainment fronts: briefing and middle tier refresh — November 7, 2026 (America/Chicago)
 
 Commits:
diff --git a/src/myNewspaper.Web/wwwroot/mobile.css b/src/myNewspaper.Web/wwwroot/mobile.css
index 83418e9..4f659ac 100644
--- a/src/myNewspaper.Web/wwwroot/mobile.css
+++ b/src/myNewspaper.Web/wwwroot/mobile.css
@@ -1922,6 +1922,8 @@ span.mx-stepper__btn {
     line-height: 1.3;
     text-decoration: none;
 }
+/* The Tech week summary reuses the tile grid outside .nf-brief--phone; the fronts' briefings get two per row from
+   .nf-brief--phone in sections.css. */
 .pub .tc-brief--phone .nf-brief__tiles {
     grid-auto-flow: row;
     grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
diff --git a/src/myNewspaper.Web/wwwroot/sections.css b/src/myNewspaper.Web/wwwroot/sections.css
index b3d2cb5..b44ff99 100644
--- a/src/myNewspaper.Web/wwwroot/sections.css
+++ b/src/myNewspaper.Web/wwwroot/sections.css
@@ -3021,17 +3021,27 @@ a.tvc-row__show:hover {
     padding: 12px 16px;
     background: var(--surface);
 }
-/* Phones: three tiles share the width, so the small print wraps rather than cutting off. */
+/* Phones: two tiles per row on every front, the way Tech has always done it. An odd tile out at the end takes the
+   whole row, so Sports' five and Entertainment's three end on one wide tile instead of a gap. */
+.pub .nf-brief--phone .nf-brief__tiles {
+    grid-auto-flow: row;
+    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
+}
+.pub .nf-brief--phone .nf-tile:last-child:nth-child(odd) {
+    grid-column: 1 / -1;
+}
 .pub .nf-brief--phone .nf-tile {
     padding: 10px;
 }
 .pub .nf-brief--phone .nf-tile__sub {
     white-space: normal;
 }
-@media (max-width: 359px) {
-    .pub .nf-brief--phone .nf-tile__value {
-        font-size: 22px;
-    }
+/* Word values ("Brazilian Grand Prix", "Election") break between words; the size eases down on narrow phones so a
+   long word still fits half the width. */
+.pub .nf-brief--phone .nf-tile__value {
+    overflow-wrap: break-word;
+    font-size: clamp(20px, 6vw, 26px);
+    line-height: 1.05;
 }
 .pub .nf-opinion .ef-headlines__title {
     font-family: var(--font-serif);
